1. $P(\text{first is heart})=\frac{13}{52}=\frac{1}{4}$.
2. After a heart is removed, 51 cards remain. There are 4 kings in the deck, but if the first heart was the king of hearts, only 3 kings remain.
- Case 1: first card is the king of hearts (probability $\frac{1}{52}$). Then $P(\text{second is king}\mid\text{first is K♥})=\frac{3}{51}$.
- Case 2: first card is a non‑king heart (probability $\frac{12}{52}$). Then $P(\text{second is king}\mid\text{first is non‑king heart})=\frac{4}{51}$.
Combine using the law of total probability:
\[
P = \frac{1}{52}\cdot\frac{3}{51} + \frac{12}{52}\cdot\frac{4}{51}
= \frac{3}{2652} + \frac{48}{2652}
= \frac{51}{2652}
= \frac{1}{52}
\]
Thus the probability is $\boxed{\frac{1}{52}}$.

Use the addition rule with overlap.
\[
P(M\cup P)=\frac{|M|+|P|-|M\cap P|}{30}
